add(("p", r"The three outcomes of Section 2.2 behave differently (Table 4 and Fig. 4). The minimum of $I(75)$ is at $u = %.2f$, and the metabolic age computed from it falls from 75 to %.1f years, a reduction of %.1f years. The minimum of SIM, and of the metabolic age computed from it, is at $u = %.2f$, and that age falls to %.1f years, a reduction of %.1f years, %.1f times larger. The difference arises because SIM contains the direct glucose-lowering effect of the agent: glucose falls at once when treatment starts, which makes SIM drop abruptly (Fig. 4B), whereas information loss responds only through the damage rate, over decades. At the largest dose SIM ends above its untreated value, which happens when the proportional gain in muscle deficit exceeds the proportional fall in glucose, while the information loss ends %.4f above its untreated value. Both readouts register the backfire at large dose, but most of the benefit that SIM reports at its optimum is not a benefit in the model's own definition of aging." % (USTAR, AGE_I_OPT, 75 - AGE_I_OPT, ARGMIN_SIM_REF, AGE_SIM_OPT, 75 - AGE_SIM_OPT, (75 - AGE_SIM_OPT) / (75 - AGE_I_OPT), I75_UMAX - I75_0)))
